{"name":"Witch Hazel Leaf","hex":"#8e8976","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"witch","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A person (now usually particularly a woman) who uses magical or supernatural powers to influence or predict events, particularly one with malicious motives.\n\n A person of any gender who harmfully employs magical power (especially innate rather than ritual)","A person of any gender who harmfully employs magical power (especially innate rather than ritual)"]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To dowse for water."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/witch"},{"term":"hazel","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A tree or shrub of the genus Corylus, bearing edible nuts called hazelnuts or filberts.","The nut of the hazel tree."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a greenish-brown colour. (often used to refer to eye colour)"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/hazel"},{"term":"leaf","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The usually green and flat organ that represents the most prominent feature of most vegetative plants.","A foliage leaf or any of the many and often considerably different structures it can specialise into."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To produce leaves; put forth foliage.","To divide (a vegetable) into separate leaves."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/leaf"}],"entries":[{"title":"Witch-hazel","blocks":[{"text":"Witch-hazels or witch hazels (Hamamelis) are a genus of flowering plants in the family Hamamelidaceae, with three species in North America, and one each in Japan (H. japonica) and China (H. mollis). The North American species are occasionally called winterbloom."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/aa/Hamamelis_virginiana_-_K%C3%B6hler%E2%80%93s_Medizinal-Pflanzen-070.jpg/330px-Hamamelis_virginiana_-_K%C3%B6hler%E2%80%93s_Medizinal-Pflanzen-070.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Witch-hazel"},{"title":"Witchcraft","blocks":[{"text":"Witchcraft is the use of magic by a person called a witch. Traditionally, \"witchcraft\" means the use of magic to inflict supernatural harm or misfortune on others, and this remains the most common and widespread meaning. Though the idea of witchcraft is largely imaginary, it has nevertheless served in many cultures as a way to explain the presence of evil. The belief in witches has been found throughout history in a great number of societies worldwide. Most of these societies have used protective magic or counter-magic against witchcraft, and have shunned, banished, imprisoned, physically punished or killed alleged witches. Anthropologists use the term \"witchcraft\" for similar beliefs about harmful occult practices in different cultures, and these societies often use the term when speaking in English."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f8/John_William_Waterhouse_-_Magic_Circle.JPG/330px-John_William_Waterhouse_-_Magic_Circle.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Witchcraft"},{"title":"Hazel","blocks":[{"text":"Hazels are plants of the genus Corylus of deciduous trees and large shrubs native to the temperate Northern Hemisphere. The genus is usually placed in the birch family, Betulaceae, though some botanists split the hazels into a separate family Corylaceae. The fruit of the hazel is the hazelnut; the trees are also grown as ornaments in hedges or gardens."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b0/Corylus_avellana_0001.JPG/330px-Corylus_avellana_0001.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hazel"},{"title":"Leaf","blocks":[{"text":"A leaf is a principal appendage of the stem of a vascular plant, usually borne laterally above ground and specialized for photosynthesis. Leaves are collectively called foliage, as in \"autumn foliage\", while the leaves, stem, flower, and fruit collectively form the shoot system. In most leaves, the primary photosynthetic tissue is the palisade mesophyll and is located on the upper side of the blade or lamina of the leaf, but in some species, including the mature foliage of Eucalyptus, palisade mesophyll is present on both sides and the leaves are said to be isobilateral. The leaf is an integral part of the stem system, and most leaves are flattened and have distinct upper (adaxial) and lower (abaxial) surfaces that differ in color, hairiness, the number of stomata, the amount and structure of epicuticular wax, and other features. Leaves are mostly green in color due to the presence of a compound called chlorophyll, which is essential for photosynthesis as it absorbs light energy from the Sun. A leaf with lighter-colored or white patches or edges is called a variegated leaf."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c7/Lisc_lipy.jpg/330px-Lisc_lipy.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Leaf"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8c/Hamamelis_flowers.jpg/250px-Hamamelis_flowers.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8c/Hamamelis_flowers.jpg/500px-Hamamelis_flowers.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8c/Hamamelis_flowers.jpg/250px-Hamamelis_flowers.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHamamelis_flowers.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4a/Hamamelis_Flower.jpg/250px-Hamamelis_Flower.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_con